This is probably where I get most of my false bug reports from -- from
the "I don't remember it in vanilla, therefore it MUST be new in
Spork!" crowd.  That mindset isn't a good way to approach things.

Vanilla is a very complex animal, with a large number of corner-case
interactions; after all, TDTTOE, right?  Spork adds complexity, without
a doubt, and there are certainly cases where the complexity I introduce
_will_ create bugs... but not all cases are like that, and it's
important to distinguish which.

The human memory is a fickle thing; it'll fabricate things from nowhere,
and it can lead us to believe something happens "every time" when in
fact it happens less than half... it can even lose track of things, so
that we don't remember things that _did_ happen, but rarely.

I only have a finite amount of spare time; I work a full-time job and
have a girlfriend whom I live with, and while she's very understanding
about the time I'm spending with this, I have to be careful and try to
balance things as best I can.  What this really means is:  If you think 
you've found a bug, the best way to demonstrate it is to provide some 
kind of recreate steps, or at least recurring instances of the bug 
occurring in similar circumstances.  By the same token, the best way to 
get me to skim over the report is if the only support you provide is "I've 
never seen it in vanilla." :/  I just don't have the time available to
go on speculative hunts through the code, sorry.

As far as the above... in wizmode, I ate a tin of baby green dragon 
meat while I wasn't hallucinating and it reported the contents correctly.
I then quaffed a potion of hallucination and it reported the contents of
the second tin as smelling like gray fungi, while it was still baby
green dragon when I ate it.  This is unchanged behavior from vanilla, 
to the best of my knowledge; so I'm going to move on and work on
something else.

